3	// Recovery of a submit record's payload must not depend on the casing of its
4	// JSON field name. encoding/json matches struct fields case-insensitively, so a
5	// checksum-valid record that spells the field PAYLOAD, Payload or a mixed case
6	// still populates the same struct value as one using the conventional spelling;
7	// the plain/base64 presence split that protects message content must apply the
8	// same folding. These tests pin:
9	//
10	//   - a record carrying exactly one payload representation restores the same
11	//     bytes under every casing — plain text is read as saved text and a sole
12	//     base64 field restores its original bytes byte-for-byte (never the U+FFFD
13	//     replacement rune for invalid UTF-8);
14	//   - the restored bytes then take part in ordinary same-id comparison: an
15	//     identical resubmit of an unterminated message returns its record, while a
16	//     one-byte payload change is ErrConflict and keeps the original;
17	//   - both representations present in ANY casing combination — one empty, both
18	//     empty, or decoding to identical bytes — is corruption, as is a sole
19	//     undecodable base64 field under a re-cased key; the two verdicts keep
20	//     distinct wording, the open returns ErrCorrupt with no usable queue, and a
21	//     complete offending frame is never dropped as a torn tail even when normal
22	//     records precede it;
23	//   - the legal empty-payload shapes (both keys omitted, sole empty plain, sole
24	//     empty base64), re-cased keys included, still mean the empty payload.
25	
